---
title: Customize & Regenerate Theme
---

Now that you know how `config/theme.json` is structured, it's time to edit it in your local clone and regenerate the Compose theme resources the app actually reads.

## Edit a Token

In your local clone of `vonage-video-android-app`, open `config/theme.json` and change one or more tokens under `colors`, `borderRadius`, or `typography`. For example, update the primary brand color for both light and dark mode, and increase the medium border radius:

```json
{
  "themes": {
    "vonage": {
      "colors": {
        "light": { "primary": "#1D6FE6", "on-primary": "#FFFFFF" },
        "dark":  { "primary": "#5FA0FF", "on-primary": "#000000" }
      },
      "borderRadius": {
        "medium": 10
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Save the file once you've made your edit.

## Regenerate the Theme Resources

From the root of your local clone, run the same config generator Gradle task used for feature flags — it also regenerates the theme:

```sh
./gradlew generateVonageConfig
```

The `ThemeGeneratorPlugin` reads `config/theme.json` and (re)generates the Compose theme resources into:

```
vonage-video-ui-compose/src/main/java/com/vonage/android/compose/theme/
```

{% aside type="caution" %}
Do **not** edit the generated theme files under `vonage-video-ui-compose/.../theme/` directly — they are overwritten on every build. Always make your changes in `config/theme.json` instead.
{% /aside %}

## Rebuild the App

```sh
./gradlew installDebug
```

Or in Android Studio, press the ▶️ **Run** button (or **Shift+F10**) with your emulator or device selected.

Next, you'll confirm your changes in the running app.
